# Message templates

Generated once by nise; owned by this application. Nise will not overwrite
anything in this directory.

The files here carry no ownership header of their own, and this README is the
declaration for all of them. A header in `invitation.txt` would be sent
verbatim to a person, because the plain-text part of a message is its body
with nothing stripped — so the ownership marker lives in the directory rather
than in each file, the same resolution ADR 0009 applies to files with no
comment syntax at all.

## The rules

Templates come in pairs sharing a base name: a `.txt` rendered with
`text/template`, and a `.html` rendered with `html/template`. Both are
embedded in the binary and parsed once, at startup, so a typo is a deployment
failure rather than something the first affected user discovers.

- **The `.txt` is required.** A message with only an HTML part is filtered
  more aggressively, unreadable in a text client, and unreadable to a screen
  reader that has given up on the markup. A `.txt` with no `.html` sibling is
  refused at startup.
- **The two engines are different on purpose.** `text/template` does no
  escaping, which is right for a body that is not markup and catastrophic for
  one that is. `html/template` escapes contextually: a name containing a `<`
  arrives as a name, and a `javascript:` URL never survives into an `href`.
- **The subject is not in the template.** It is passed to `Render`, so it can
  be localized, tested, and read without a convention about which line of the
  file it is.
- **Every key a template uses must be present in its data.** Templates are
  parsed with `missingkey=error`, so a missing key fails the render rather
  than putting `<no value>` into a message somebody receives.

See [`docs/mail.md`](../../../../../docs/mail.md) in the framework repository
for the surrounding contract.
